# Flight Finder — Complete Rebuild Plan

Your vision is genuinely ambitious and the existing codebase has solid architectural bones. The backend pipeline (dispatcher → workers → coupon brute-forcer → deal scorer) is well-structured, and the ML prediction system is surprisingly sophisticated. However, the current app has critical integration gaps that prevent it from actually running end-to-end, and the frontend — while functional — doesn't match the premium experience your idea deserves.

**I'm proposing a from-scratch frontend rebuild** with targeted backend fixes to make the entire system actually boot up, run searches, and deliver results. Here's what changes and why.

---

## User Review Required

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **Dropping the Electron Shell**: The current Electron wrapper adds complexity (spawn timing, window management, preload scripts) but provides zero features you can't get from a browser. I recommend running this as a **pure web app** — `npm run dev` for the frontend, `python main.py` for the backend. This eliminates the #1 failure mode (Electron not waiting for Python to boot). Do you agree, or do you want to keep Electron?

> [!IMPORTANT]
> **API Keys**: Your `.env` has Gemini API keys for the AI Worker. The AI Worker is the real-site scraping engine (MakeMyTrip, Cleartrip, etc.) that uses LLM to navigate pages dynamically. For the initial build, I'll make the **MockWorker** (simulated airline site) work flawlessly so you can demo the full pipeline without needing live API keys or risking anti-bot detection. The real-site workers will work once you have valid API keys and the mock pipeline is proven. Sound good?

> [!WARNING]
> **No actual flight booking will happen** — the app navigates to checkout and tests coupons, but the "Book Now" handoff feature (bringing hidden browser to foreground) requires headed Playwright browsers. I'll make this work with the mock worker pipeline. Real-site booking handoff is a Phase 2 feature that depends on residential proxies and stealth browser config.

